<p>Web excursions brought to you in partnership with CleanShot X, the absolute, hands-down best app for Mac screenshots. <a href="https://cleanshot.sjv.io/DKRzyd">Get one of my all-time favorite apps here.</a></p>

<dl>
  <dt><a href="https://yemcube.tech/lidtap">LidTap</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>LidTap makes your MacBook screen respond to a tap, with no touchscreen and no extra hardware. Free for MacBooks with M2 or later, and M1 Pro or M1 Max MacBook Pro.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dt><a href="https://getfinis.com/">Finis</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Write your book on Mac and iPad with Finis and Virgil, your AI editorial assistant. Organize, revise, decide what stays. 14 days free, then $34.90 / €39.90.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>I really like the very minimal editor, and the fact that everything is stored as Markdown you can open in Marked or anywhere. Way less overwhelming than Scrivener, if you don&rsquo;t need everything Scrivener offers.</p>
  </dd>
  <dt><a href="https://www.autonomous.ai/lamp?utm_source=brettterpstra&amp;utm_medium=newsletter&amp;utm_campaign=lamp_prebf_2026&amp;utm_content=editorial">Autonomous Lamp</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Autonomous Lamp is a pre-assembled desktop AI companion. It sees, moves, talks, does real tasks with 100+ skills, and writes new ones you describe.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>If I had the money to spend on this, I&rsquo;m certain I could find space on my desk for it.</p>
  </dd>
  <dt><a href="https://coffeetext.app/">Coffee</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>A quiet Markdown editor for the Mac. Plain files in a folder you own, with instant answers as you write, search and on-device AI. €3 a month, 14 days free.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>I am literally seething with jealousy that this beat me to market. I&rsquo;ve been planning out my ultimate Markdown editor, and then along comes Coffee. It does everything I could want, including sidecar comments, Git integration, inline Soulver-like conversions, dates, and math, inline Markdown conversion, PDF and EPUB export, the works. I can&rsquo;t imagine Markdown writers not finding it worth US$4/month.</p>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>It can even do DOCX conversion to Markdown almost as well as Marked does (but not better, I don&rsquo;t <em>think</em>).</p>
  </dd>
  <dt><a href="https://feelbits.gitlab.io/">Feelbits</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Check in with how you feel. Each feeling becomes a little Feelbit in your garden. Free on iPhone, and everything stays on your phone.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>I have a really, really bad memory. When things seem grim, I can&rsquo;t remember them ever <em>not</em> feeling grim. And when things feel happy, you could convince me I&rsquo;ve always felt happy. This is why I love mood tracking apps. I have no issue with them being cute, like Feelbits here. I generally rely on <a href="https://exist.io">Exist</a>, which has great cumulative reports and correlations, but I will gladly try a new one now and then.</p>
  </dd>
</dl>

<p>I do all of my screenshots and screen recordings with CleanShot X. I love it to pieces. <a href="https://cleanshot.sjv.io/DKRzyd">You should get it.</a></p>

